        "# Lab Report: Sequential Data Prediction Using Autoregressive (AR) Models and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) Networks\n",
        "**ETC3123 Artificial Intelligence for Engineering Applications: Graded Open-Ended Lab 3**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Prepared by:** Oh Yu Qiao (24013567)\n",
        "\n",
        "**Objective:** This lab explores sequential data prediction in an engineering context by evaluating the performance of a traditional Autoregressive (AR) model and a modern Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) neural network on daily temperature data.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Abstract\n",
        "This report compares an autoregressive (AR) model and a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network for one-day-ahead forecasting of daily mean temperature in Delhi (2013–2017). Exploratory analysis (ADF test, ACF/PACF, seasonal decomposition) guided the choice of AR order and LSTM window length, and both models were tuned on a chronological validation set using controlled, multi-seed experiments. On the held-out 2017 test set, AR(11) achieved an RMSE of 1.700 °C and the LSTM 1.707–1.878 °C depending on how it was retrained, while a naive persistence forecast (tomorrow = today) achieved 1.684 °C. Both models showed a one-day phase lag, amplitude dampening and almost identical error patterns (residual correlation 0.971). For this short-horizon, linearly dominated problem, the simpler AR model gives the same accuracy as the LSTM at a small fraction of the computational cost."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "rev_md_002"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 1. Background and Literature Review\n",
        "\n",
        "The lecture slides (Topic 3: 03a Problem Formulation, 03b Autoregressive Models, 03c Deep Learning) introduce the AR model and recurrent networks, but not most of the statistical tools used in this report to analyse the data and judge the models. This section summarises those tools: what each one measures, how to read it, and where it is used. Items marked **†** go beyond the scope of the slides.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.1 Problem formulation (Lecture 03a)\n",
        "- **Objective:** time series forecasting (regression) of one variable, daily mean temperature, so the data is a **univariate time series**.\n",
        "- **One-step-ahead prediction:** to predict day *t*, a model may use the actual observations up to day *t − 1*. Every model in this report is evaluated this way, on the same dates.\n",
        "- **Sliding window:** a fixed-length input made of the last *n* observations. For AR, *n* is the order *p*; for the LSTM, *n* is the window length `seq_len`.\n",
        "- **Temporal dependencies:** *short-term* if only the recent past matters, *long-term* if distant values still affect the next value. This determines how large *p* and `seq_len` need to be.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.2 Stationarity and the Augmented Dickey–Fuller (ADF) test **†**\n",
        "A series is **stationary** if its mean, variance and autocorrelation structure do not change over time. Lecture 03b lists stationarity as an assumption of the AR model. Trends and seasonal cycles make a series non-stationary.\n",
        "\n",
        "The ADF test (Dickey & Fuller, 1979; Said & Dickey, 1984) checks for a **unit root**, the defining property of a random walk $y_t = y_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$, in which shocks never die out.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Item | Meaning |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| Null hypothesis H₀ | The series has a unit root, i.e. it is **non-stationary** |\n",
        "| Alternative H₁ | The series is stationary |\n",
        "| ADF statistic | The more negative the statistic, the stronger the evidence against H₀ |\n",
        "| Decision rule | **p < 0.05** (equivalently, statistic < 5% critical value) → reject H₀ → stationary |\n",
        "\n",
        "Because H₀ is \"non-stationary\", a **small** p-value is the result that indicates stationarity. A non-stationary series can often be made stationary by **first differencing**, $\\Delta y_t = y_t - y_{t-1}$ (Hyndman & Athanasopoulos, 2021, §9.1).\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.3 Autocorrelation (ACF) and partial autocorrelation (PACF) **†**\n",
        "- **ACF at lag *k*:** the correlation between $y_t$ and $y_{t-k}$. It includes *indirect* effects that pass through the lags in between.\n",
        "- **PACF at lag *k*:** the correlation between $y_t$ and $y_{t-k}$ after removing the effect of lags 1 to *k − 1*, i.e. the *direct* effect of lag *k* only.\n",
        "- **Significance band:** approximately $\\pm 1.96/\\sqrt{N}$ for a series of length *N* (the shaded area in the plots). Bars outside the band are significant at the 5% level. When many lags are tested, about 5% of them will cross the band by chance alone.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Pattern | Interpretation | Used in |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| ACF starts near 1 and decays very slowly | Highly persistent or non-stationary series (trend or unit root) | §4 |\n",
        "| ACF oscillates with a fixed period *s* | Seasonality with period *s* (e.g. a peak at lag 365 for daily data) | §4 |\n",
        "| PACF cuts off sharply after lag *p* while the ACF tails off | AR(*p*) behaviour, so *p* is a candidate order | §4, §6 |\n",
        "| No significant bars | White noise: no linear structure left to model | §6 (residuals) |\n",
        "\n",
        "These identification rules come from the Box–Jenkins methodology (Box et al., 2015; Hyndman & Athanasopoulos, 2021, §9.5).\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.4 Seasonal decomposition **†**\n",
        "Classical additive decomposition writes the series as $y_t = T_t + S_t + R_t$: a **trend** (a centred moving average over one seasonal period), a repeating **seasonal** component, and a **remainder** (Hyndman & Athanasopoulos, 2021, §3.4). It shows whether non-stationarity comes from the trend, the seasonal cycle, or both. The trend is undefined for the first and last half-period, which is why it is blank at both ends of Figure 3."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
