      },
      "source": [
        "### 1.5 AR order selection and residual diagnostics\n",
        "Lecture 03b gives five steps for implementing an AR model: preprocessing, model identification, parameter estimation, model diagnostics and model validation. In addition to the ACF/PACF, this report uses the following tools.\n",
        "- **AIC and BIC †** (Akaike, 1974; Schwarz, 1978): $\\text{AIC} = -2\\ln L + 2k$ and $\\text{BIC} = -2\\ln L + k\\ln N$, where *L* is the likelihood and *k* the number of parameters. Lower is better. Both reward goodness of fit and penalise extra parameters; BIC penalises them more heavily, so it tends to choose smaller models. They can only be compared between models fitted on the **same observations**, which is why every AR order is fitted with the same `hold_back` in §6.\n",
        "- **Estimation:** `statsmodels.AutoReg` estimates the coefficients by conditional least squares, one of the two estimation methods in the slides (Seabold & Perktold, 2010).\n",
        "- **Ljung–Box test** (Ljung & Box, 1978): tests whether the residual autocorrelations up to lag *h* are jointly zero, using $Q = N(N+2)\\sum_{k=1}^{h}\\hat\\rho_k^2/(N-k)$. H₀ is \"residuals are uncorrelated (white noise)\", so **p > 0.05 is the good outcome**, the opposite direction to the ADF test. For residuals of an AR(*p*) model the degrees of freedom are reduced by *p* (`model_df=p`), and only lags *h > p* are meaningful.\n",
        "- **Coefficient check:** for AR(*p*), a coefficient sum $\\sum\\phi_i$ close to 1 means the process is close to a random walk (unit root). For a stationary AR model the implied long-run mean is $c/(1-\\sum\\phi_i)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.6 LSTM training choices **†**\n",
        "Lecture 03c explains the LSTM cell (forget, input and output gates and the cell state) and why it handles long-term dependencies better than a plain RNN, which suffers from vanishing gradients (Bengio et al., 1994; Hochreiter & Schmidhuber, 1997). The following training choices are not covered in the slides:\n",
        "- **MinMax scaling:** $x' = (x - x_{\\min})/(x_{\\max} - x_{\\min})$, fitted on training data only. The sigmoid and tanh activations inside the gates saturate for large inputs, so unscaled data slows or destabilises learning.\n",
        "- **Adam optimiser** (Kingma & Ba, 2015): gradient descent with an adaptive step size per weight. The *learning rate* sets the overall step size.\n",
        "- **Early stopping** (Prechelt, 1998): training stops when the loss on a hold-out set has not improved for `patience` epochs, and the best weights are restored. It limits overfitting, but the stopping point depends on which data is held out (see §8.2).\n",
        "- **Dropout** (Srivastava et al., 2014): randomly switches off units during training to reduce overfitting.\n",
        "- **Seed variability:** weight initialisation and batch order are random, so the same configuration gives different results with different seeds. Results are therefore reported as mean ± standard deviation over several seeds (Reimers & Gurevych, 2017).\n",
        "- **GRU** (Cho et al., 2014): the simpler gated network from Lecture 03c, with two gates and fewer parameters. It is not tested here but is listed as future work.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.7 Evaluation: metrics, baseline and tracking diagnostics\n",
        "\n",
        "| Metric | Formula | Interpretation and caveats |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| RMSE | $\\sqrt{\\tfrac{1}{n}\\sum (y_t-\\hat y_t)^2}$ | Typical error in °C; penalises large errors more heavily |\n",
        "| MAE | $\\tfrac{1}{n}\\sum \\lvert y_t-\\hat y_t\\rvert$ | Average error in °C; less sensitive to occasional large errors |\n",
        "| MAPE | $\\tfrac{100}{n}\\sum \\lvert y_t-\\hat y_t\\rvert / \\lvert y_t\\rvert$ | Error as a percentage. Unstable when values are near zero, and the same °C error counts more when temperatures are low (Hyndman & Koehler, 2006). Usable here because Delhi temperatures stay above 6 °C |\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Persistence (naive) baseline:** $\\hat y_t = y_{t-1}$ (Hyndman & Athanasopoulos, 2021, §5.2). It is the optimal forecast for a random walk. A model is only useful if it beats this baseline.\n",
        "- **Phase lag:** a forecast that reacts one step late. It is measured by correlating each prediction $\\hat y_t$ with the actual value *k* days earlier, $y_{t-k}$: if *k* = 1 gives a higher correlation than *k* = 0, the forecast is mostly repeating yesterday.\n",
        "- **Amplitude dampening:** forecasts that are smoother than reality. It is measured by the ratio std(daily changes of the forecast) / std(daily changes of the actual series); a ratio below 1 means peaks and troughs are flattened. Models trained with squared error tend to predict the conditional mean, which pulls forecasts away from extremes.\n",
        "- **Rolling-origin evaluation †** (Tashman, 2000) and the **Diebold–Mariano test †** (Diebold & Mariano, 1995) are the standard ways to make forecast comparisons more robust and to test whether a difference is statistically significant. They are not used here and are discussed as limitations.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.8 Related work **†**\n",
        "In a large comparison on 1,045 monthly series from the M3 forecasting competition, Makridakis et al. (2018) found that simple statistical methods were usually more accurate than machine learning methods, including LSTM networks, for univariate forecasting, while needing far less computation. The results of this lab are consistent with that finding."
      ]
    },
