        "**Table 3. Summary of EDA evidence**\n",
        "\n",
        "| Analysis | Observation | Implication for modelling |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| ADF test (Table 2) | Original series non-stationary (p = 0.22); first difference stationary | AR is fitted on the original series and, as a check, on the differenced series |\n",
        "| Decomposition (Figure 3) | Yearly cycle of about ±12 °C; slowly rising trend (≈ 24.5 → 27 °C) | Non-stationarity comes mainly from seasonality |\n",
        "| Short-lag ACF (Figure 4) | Very slow decay: 0.97 at lag 1, still 0.62 at lag 40 | Very high persistence, so the persistence baseline will be hard to beat |\n",
        "| Long-lag ACF (Figure 5) | Negative near lag 180, positive peak near lag 365 | Confirms yearly seasonality, which short-window models cannot see |\n",
        "| PACF (Figure 4) | Lag 1 dominant (0.972); lags 2–4 significant (0.08–0.14); smaller peaks at lags 10–11 | Main cut-off at p ≈ 4, secondary structure at p ≈ 11 |\n",
        "\n",
        "**Discussion**\n",
        "- **Stationarity:** the ADF test and the decomposition both show that daily mean temperature is non-stationary because of its yearly cycle and gradual trend. First differencing makes it stationary.\n",
        "- **Persistence:** the lag-1 partial autocorrelation of 0.972 is close to 1, the value for a random walk, so most of each day's temperature is explained by the previous day's. The persistence model ($\\hat y_t = y_{t-1}$) is therefore used as the baseline.\n",
        "- **Lag range:** the PACF suggests a main cut-off at lag 4 and weaker structure at lags 10–11. The isolated significant values at lags 21, 22, 35 and 38 only just cross the ±0.051 band; with 40 lags tested, about two such crossings are expected by chance, so they are ignored. The AR order is searched over **p = 1–30**, and LSTM windows of **7, 14 and 30 days** are tested. A 365-day window would be needed to capture the yearly cycle directly, but would leave too few training sequences."
