        "## 6. Autoregressive (AR) Model\n",
        "\n",
        "### 6.1 Method\n",
        "The AR model follows the five implementation steps of Lecture 03b: **(1) preprocessing** (§3–4), **(2) identification** of the order *p* (ACF/PACF in §4, plus the validation sweep and AIC/BIC below), **(3) parameter estimation** (least squares with `statsmodels.AutoReg`, with a constant), **(4) diagnostics** (Ljung–Box test on the residuals) and **(5) validation** (validation and test sets).\n",
        "\n",
        "To give **one-step-ahead** forecasts that are comparable with the LSTM, the coefficients learned on the training data are applied to the *actual* lagged observations:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\hat y_t = c + \\phi_1 y_{t-1} + \\phi_2 y_{t-2} + \\dots + \\phi_p y_{t-p}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The sample code in the lab sheet instead called `predict()` beyond the end of the training data, which gives a *recursive multi-step* forecast (each prediction is fed back as an input), a harder task than the LSTM's. The hand-written formula is checked against statsmodels below.\n",
        "\n",
        "Two variants are tuned over p = 1–30:\n",
        "- **AR (original):** fitted directly on daily mean temperature.\n",
        "- **AR (differenced):** fitted on $\\Delta y_t = y_t - y_{t-1}$, which the ADF test showed to be stationary; forecasts are converted back with $\\hat y_t = y_{t-1} + \\Delta\\hat y_t$.\n",
        "\n",
        "All orders are fitted on the same observations (the first 30 values are held back), so their AIC/BIC values are comparable."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# AR functions (hard coded, hand written AR equation)\n",
        "\n",
        "MAX_P = 30\n",
        "\n",
        "def fit_ar(series, p, hold_back=None):\n",
        "    \"\"\"Fit AR(p) with a constant. hold_back keeps the estimation sample identical across orders.\"\"\"\n",
        "    return AutoReg(series.to_numpy(), lags=p, trend='c', hold_back=hold_back).fit()\n",
        "\n",
        "def ar_one_step(res, history, target, p):\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    One-step-ahead AR forecasts for the dates in `target`, using coefficients fitted on\n",
        "    `history` and the ACTUAL previous observations (no recursive feedback).\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    combined = pd.concat([history, target])\n",
        "    const = res.params[0]\n",
        "    phi = res.params[1:p+1]\n",
        "    # Column i holds y_{t-(i+1)}, i.e. lag 1 ... lag p\n",
        "    lag_matrix = np.column_stack([combined.shift(i).to_numpy() for i in range(1, p+1)])\n",
        "    preds = const + lag_matrix @ phi\n",
        "    return pd.Series(preds, index=combined.index).loc[target.index]\n",
        "\n",
        "def ar_diff_one_step(res, history, target, p):\n",
        "    \"\"\"One-step forecasts from an AR model fitted on first differences, converted back to levels.\"\"\"\n",
        "    combined = pd.concat([history, target])\n",
        "    d_combined = combined.diff()\n",
        "    d_pred = ar_one_step(res, d_combined.loc[history.index].dropna(),\n",
        "                         d_combined.loc[target.index], p)\n",
        "    return combined.shift(1).loc[target.index] + d_pred\n",
        "\n",
        "# ---- Sanity check: our manual formula must reproduce statsmodels' own fitted values ----\n",
        "_res = fit_ar(y_tr, 5)\n",
        "_manual = ar_one_step(_res, y_tr.iloc[:0], y_tr, 5).iloc[5:].to_numpy()\n",
        "print(\"Manual AR formula matches statsmodels:\", np.allclose(_manual, _res.fittedvalues))"
      ],
