        "## 9. Discussion\n",
        "\n",
        "### 9.1 Linear (AR) vs non-linear (LSTM) dynamics\n",
        "In theory the LSTM is the more expressive model: it is non-linear and, through its gates and cell state, can learn long-term dependencies (Lecture 03c). In practice it gave no benefit over the linear AR(11), for four reasons:\n",
        "1. **The dominant structure is linear persistence.** The lag-1 partial autocorrelation is 0.972 and the AR coefficients sum to 0.987, close to a random walk. A linear model already captures this.\n",
        "2. **Little structure was left to learn.** The AR(11) residuals pass the Ljung–Box test (Table 7), and the AR and LSTM test errors are almost identical (r = 0.971). If the LSTM had found real non-linear patterns, its errors would differ from AR's.\n",
        "3. **The dependencies are short-term**, so the LSTM's main advantage over AR and plain RNNs (long-term memory without vanishing gradients) is not needed. The best window was only 7 days, consistent with the PACF and with p = 11.\n",
        "4. **The problem is simple and the data is small.** Increasing the units 14-fold did not change the error, and a second layer made it worse (Table 9). With about 1,170 training sequences from one variable, extra capacity mainly adds overfitting risk and seed variability.\n",
        "\n",
        "Both models also see the **same information**, past temperature only. The largest errors come from weather events (rain, cloud, cold air) that are not in the input, and a non-linear model cannot recover information that is not there. This agrees with Makridakis et al. (2018), who found that statistical methods often match or beat machine learning methods for univariate forecasting.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 9.2 Phase lag and amplitude dampening\n",
        "**Phase lag.** All models lag by one day (Table 11, Figure 16). This is not a coding error but a consequence of the task: for a highly persistent series, the forecast that minimises squared error given data up to *t − 1* is very close to $y_{t-1}$, and a sudden change on day *t* cannot be seen until the next step. Two implications follow:\n",
        "- **A low RMSE does not mean useful foresight.** An RMSE of about 1.7 °C looks good on its own, but persistence achieves it with no model at all. Without the baseline, the AR and LSTM results would look more impressive than they are.\n",
        "- **Lag plus trend creates bias.** During the 2017 warming, every lagging forecast under-predicts, by about one day's rise for persistence and more for models that also smooth.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Amplitude dampening.** Both models are smoother than reality (daily-change ratio 0.78 for AR, 0.61 for the early-stopped LSTM). Three mechanisms contribute: (1) squared-error loss makes models predict the conditional mean, which lies between possible outcomes; (2) AR coefficients summing to slightly less than 1 pull forecasts towards the long-run mean; (3) under-training made the LSTM smoother still, and full training raised its ratio to 0.75. In engineering terms, dampening matters because **systems are often designed for the extremes**: a cooling-load or heat-warning model built on these forecasts would underestimate the hottest days.\n",
        "\n",
        "Reducing both effects needs information about *upcoming* changes, for example humidity, wind or pressure inputs, weather forecasts, or seasonal features such as day of year.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 9.3 Robustness and the validation–test gap\n",
        "- **Seasonal shift:** validation (March–December 2016) covered the hot season and the autumn cooling, while the test period (January–April 2017) is cold and volatile, then warms quickly. Both models beat persistence by about 5% on validation but not on test, so small gains measured in one season do not necessarily transfer to another.\n",
        "- **Reproducibility:** AR is deterministic. The LSTM's test RMSE depended on the seed (std up to 0.185 °C) and on the retraining protocol (1.878 vs 1.707 °C), a difference as large as any architecture choice in Table 9. For deep learning models, reproducibility requires fixing the whole training procedure, not just the seeds.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 9.4 Deployment constraints\n",
        "\n",
        "**Table 12. Deployment comparison**\n",
        "\n",
        "| Factor | AR(11) | LSTM |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| Test RMSE (consistent training) | 1.700 °C | 1.707 ± 0.065 °C |\n",
        "| Parameters | 12 | 1,169 (≈ 100×) |\n",
        "| Training time | 0.0125 s | ≈ 42 s per run (≈ 3,400×) |\n",
        "| Hyperparameter tuning | Seconds (60 fits) | ≈ 1 hour (48 runs) |\n",
        "| Inference time (114 days) | 0.003 s | 0.036 s |\n",
